1
00:00:01,154 --> 00:00:02,914
Hi, I'm Jang.

2
00:00:03,214 --> 00:00:07,854
This is the third video about the DESIGN:OS 3D Blender repo.

3
00:00:08,154 --> 00:00:11,914
This time: a six-servo robot arm you can 3D print.

4
00:00:13,598 --> 00:00:16,718
Before we start, six words you will hear again.

5
00:00:17,018 --> 00:00:20,698
A servo is a small motor that turns to the angle it is told.

6
00:00:20,998 --> 00:00:24,118
A joint is where two parts of the arm rotate around each other.

7
00:00:24,418 --> 00:00:29,058
A collision is two parts taking the same space, measured in cubic millimetres.

8
00:00:29,358 --> 00:00:32,718
A gate is a script that answers pass or fail.

9
00:00:33,018 --> 00:00:37,578
A plate is the printer bed where parts are laid out to print in one go.

10
00:00:37,878 --> 00:00:42,518
And a frame is one picture of the film; this film runs at twenty-four frames per second.

11
00:00:47,376 --> 00:00:52,016
This time the input is not a photo but a fifty-eight-second assembly video.

12
00:00:52,316 --> 00:00:57,276
I chose it as the baseline and changed one thing: remove the separate black power box.

13
00:01:05,154 --> 00:01:12,354
Thirty-eight parts on five plates: thirty-two in PETG, six in flexible TPU.

14
00:01:12,654 --> 00:01:15,134
The print bed is two hundred and twenty millimetres.

15
00:01:15,434 --> 00:01:20,394
And one requirement that is not lowered: the arm must lift two hundred and fifty grams for several minutes.

16
00:01:30,043 --> 00:01:36,123
Each part is turned to the orientation that needs the least support, then laid on the bed eight millimetres apart.

17
00:01:36,423 --> 00:01:40,263
The gate runs eight hundred and thirty-six checks and none fail.

18
00:01:47,821 --> 00:01:53,021
Try to recall: what was the input, how many parts, and which requirement is not lowered?

19
00:01:58,021 --> 00:02:02,501
An assembly video, thirty-eight parts, and the two-hundred-fifty-gram load.

20
00:02:10,932 --> 00:02:15,492
The assembly film has two hundred and fifty-two units and one hundred and twenty-one steps.

21
00:02:15,792 --> 00:02:20,192
Parts on the same joint are grouped so they move as one block.

22
00:02:28,710 --> 00:02:34,310
The first version had a bug: the shoulder and elbow outputs arrived up to one hundred and ten millimetres off.

23
00:02:34,610 --> 00:02:38,530
Guess: rebuild the model, or only fix the motion path?

24
00:02:42,530 --> 00:02:46,850
Only the motion path, on thirty-seven frames. The model stays the same.

25
00:02:55,376 --> 00:03:00,656
Later I watched the draft myself and saw two parts overlap at second twenty-four.

26
00:03:00,956 --> 00:03:06,076
A frame-by-frame check confirmed it: at frame five hundred and eighty-eight, two surfaces cut into each other.

27
00:03:06,376 --> 00:03:10,456
The running render was stopped, and the frames already rendered were thrown away.

28
00:03:18,487 --> 00:03:21,687
With the waiting spot fixed, the part flew straight to its target.

29
00:03:21,987 --> 00:03:24,547
Guess: is the straight path safe?

30
00:03:28,547 --> 00:03:31,107
No. It cut across the real servo cases.

31
00:03:31,407 --> 00:03:36,767
The fix: lift one hundred and twenty millimetres, move across, then lower straight down.

32
00:03:45,154 --> 00:03:48,514
Then all three thousand and thirty-one frames were checked again.

33
00:03:48,814 --> 00:03:51,214
No collision with the real servo cases remains.

34
00:04:01,154 --> 00:04:04,834
The last bug is smaller: a fingertip was cut out of the frame.

35
00:04:05,134 --> 00:04:11,454
Widen the shot eight percent, keep two thousand nine hundred and thirty-one old frames, re-render only eighty-nine.

36
00:04:18,932 --> 00:04:23,252
Try to recall: why the straight path fails, and what is safe?

37
00:04:28,252 --> 00:04:33,532
Because it cuts through a servo case. Safe is lift, move across, then lower.

38
00:04:42,043 --> 00:04:48,123
The final film runs two minutes six seconds, three thousand and twenty frames, and decodes fully without errors.

39
00:05:10,487 --> 00:05:13,447
The gate reports all eight hundred and thirty-six checks passing.

40
00:05:13,747 --> 00:05:16,627
Guess: so can it be printed and used now?

41
00:05:20,627 --> 00:05:26,547
Not yet. That gate ran with a sample spec, not the arm's own, so wall thickness was not checked.

42
00:05:26,847 --> 00:05:30,767
The two-hundred-fifty-gram load is untested. Manufacture stays blocked.

43
00:05:38,932 --> 00:05:40,372
Three things to remember.

44
00:05:44,972 --> 00:05:49,212
One, fix what is broken: the motion path, not the model.

45
00:05:49,512 --> 00:05:53,992
Two, a straight path is not safe: lift, move across, lower.

46
00:05:54,292 --> 00:05:58,212
Three, a pass only means something within the spec it checked.

47
00:06:05,598 --> 00:06:11,358
This whole arm lives in the open-source DESIGN:OS 3D Blender repo.

48
00:06:11,658 --> 00:06:13,578
I'm Jang, see you next time.
